  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   The Erd\u0151s overlap problem seeks a step function h minimizing the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. This formulation is non-convex, making it challenging for gradient-based methods.  \n   Instead of Nelder-Mead, we employ PSO for its global exploration capabilities. PSO balances exploration and exploitation by maintaining a swarm of candidate solutions, each representing a potential h.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   - Variables: h \u2208 [0,1]\u207f, n \u2208 \u2115  \n   - Objective: min C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx  \n   - Constraints: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1, h(x) \u2208 [0,1]  \n   - Discretization: h_i represents h(x) over [i\u0394x, (i+1)\u0394x), \u0394x=2/n  \n   - Feasibility: After each swarm update, clamp h_i to [0,1] and scale to meet the sum constraint.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   a. Initialization  \n      - Swarm size = 40  \n      - Initialize particles around the initial_h_values, if provided, with small random perturbations.  \n      - Each particle's velocity is initialized to small random values.  \n\n   b. Swarm updates  \n      - For each particle, compute its objective C\u2085 via cross-correlation.  \n      - Update particle velocities:  \n        v = \u03c9*v + c\u2081r\u2081(pbest - current) + c\u2082r\u2082(gbest - current)  \n        where \u03c9=0.7, c\u2081=2.0, c\u2082=2.0, r\u2081, r\u2082 ~ U(0,1)  \n      - Clip velocity to \u00b15% of current h range to prevent overshooting.  \n\n   c. Constraint handling  \n      - After velocity update:  \n         h = current + velocity  \n         h = project_to_feasible(h)  \n      - project_to_feasible:  \n         1. Clip h_i to [0,1]  \n         2. Scale to ensure sum(h) = n/2:  \n            If sum deviates by more than 1e-6, scale all elements by (n/2) / sum  \n\u751f\u4ea7\u529b",
